    {
      "tipo": "explicacion",
      "titulo": {
        "en": "Describing character precisely"
      },
      "texto": {
        "en": "**Common adjective opposites.** Some adjectives form an opposite with a prefix, but there is no single prefix that works for every word: **paciente / impaciente**, **seguro / inseguro**, **agradable / desagradable**. The spelling of **in-** changes to **im-** before **p**, as in **impaciente**. Other contrasts use **mal-** or a different word: **educado / maleducado**, **tranquilo / nervioso**. Learn the common pair for each adjective.\n\n**Tener carácter** means to have a strong personality; **tener mucho carácter** can describe someone forceful or strong-willed, without saying whether that is good or bad. **Tener mal carácter** means to be bad-tempered. **Tener sentido del humor** means to have a sense of humour.\n\nSome adjectives have a related noun: **paciente → la paciencia**; **optimista → el optimismo**; **sincero/a → la sinceridad**.\n\n**Behaviour:** **portarse bien / mal**; **tratar bien / mal a alguien**.\n\n**Adjective agreement.** Adjectives agree with the person: **Marta es introvertida; Elena es habladora.**\n\n**Parecerse a** means to look like or take after someone, in appearance or character: **Se parece a su madre; nos parecemos mucho.**\n\n**Ser** usually presents a characteristic: **Marta suele ser paciente**. **Estar** can describe a state at a particular time: **Hoy está impaciente**. The choice also depends on the adjective and context."
      }
    },
